const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"Scalar and vector\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eScalar and vector are two important concepts not only in physics but in math as well. In this section, we'll briefly explain how these two work differently and the most basic computation rules of them.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWhat is a scalar? Scalar is a physical quantity with only magnitude but no direction. Magnitude is the assigned value (the amount of the physical quantity). Examples of scalar are temperature, height, length, weight... Any physical quantity without direction is considered as a scalar, since only value itself (magnitude) changes but there's no direction change. The listed examples all share this commonality: temperature is a measure of the extent (degree) of molecular kinetic energy (which is another scalar, but energy is a very annoying concept, let's ignore it here); you never see any arrows indicating height (techniquely you can, but it's unnecessary) on a contour map in geography class. As for time, we treat it as a scalar (even though we feel that time goes forward, yet this direction can be quantified as a relatively increasing time, 1s, 2s, 3s...). Something noticeable is that any change of scalar will still be a scalar, which makes a lot of sense. The rate of change of scalar is scalar.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThen what about a vector? A vector is a promax version of a scalar. Previously, a scalar can only possess magnitude; a vector is different, it possesses both a magnitude and a direction. What are the examples of vector, then? Umm, it's hard to answer at current stage since we're still at the beginning (front door) of kinematics and motion. Yet we can still mention a few of them: velocity (the rate of change of position, NOT speed!), acceleration (the rate of change of velocity), force (the rate of change of momentum), momentum, pressure, etc. So, the change of a vector includes not only the change of its magnitude but also its direction (sometimes can be either one of them, sometimes can be both). Vectors help us to handle more sophisticated anlysis of physical world which cannot be provided with scalar alone. Therefore, the change of vector is still a vector, and the rate of change of a vector is still a vector (we'll explain this later).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eTo better explain how the two types of change of vector are, let's figure it out in 1D, 2D, and 3D scenarios. The change of magnitude is easier to understand, as it's simply the length of the vector shrinks or expands; as for direction, it's not complicated neither: on 1D axis, the direction of a vector can only be either forward or backward, so that the direction change is always opposing the original direction of the vector; in 2D plane, the direction of a vector forms a full circle on that plane, so the change of direction is always in that plane; in 3D space, the direction of a vector forms a full sphere so that the change of direction is always one direction that's included by that sphere.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnother important property of vector is that a vector never changes unless its magnitude or direction changes (this is exactly what we've talked about already). Thus, a translational motion of a vector won't change the vector. And I guess I haven't formally defined a vector mathematically. So, a vector is a directed line segment which has a starting point and an end point. The magnitude is determined by the starting point and end point regardless of the coordinate system being used (so that a vector can only change its direction or magnitude, yet this still makes it become a new vector), and the direction is usually determined by the angle between the line the vector represents and the horizontal axis (normally x-axis, measuring this angle in 2D plane needs to translate the vector's starting point onto the origin, or you simply use the formula \\\\tan(\\\\frac{v_{y}}{v_{x}})).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eNow let me introduce the computation rules of them, respectively.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor scalar, the computation rule is simple, since it's the computation of one variable of input (the physical quantity). The rule of four basic computations work as usual: addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. Addition and subtraction are viewed as the change of scalar, which is still scalar; the multiplication's and division's result is also scalar (the product of length is area, which is still a scalar (some may argue there's area vector, but this is not a formal name of it, we'll discuss it when we encounter Gauss's law); change of temperature over time is still a scalar without any indication of direction. If you've learned multi-variable calculus, you probably know vector-valued functions. Normally, a function is with multiple input variables and one single output variable, but a vector-valued function possesses multiple outputs so that each output is responsible for one dimensional component of the vector. You do not have to know this by now, as this is an explanation help those who've learned things beyond this and want a connection.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor vector, the computation is quite different from scalar. The basic principle is that when operating addition and subtraction, the same variable adds up. Usually, we write a vector \\\\overrightarrow{v} = \\u0026lt;v_{x},v_{y},v{z},...\\u0026gt;, or \\\\overrightarrow{v}=v_{x}\\\\widehat{i}+v_{y}\\\\widehat{j}+v_{z}\\\\widehat{k}+... In these two expressions, coefficients like v_{x} represents the component of the vector along one direction/dimenion/axis. In Newtonian mechanics, we usually encounter scenarios from 1D to 3D. And \\\\widehat{i}, \\\\widehat{j}, and \\\\widehat{k} are something we named as unit vector. Consider that vectors can be projected onto different dimensional axises so that the component along that direction/dimension is measured by reference to that axis projected upon. Therefore, you will need to define the unit length which forms the unit vector along that direction. Yet unit vector can also exist along the direction of the vector: divide the vector by its magnitude, \\\\overrightarrow{u_{v}}=\\u0026lt;\\\\frac{v_{x}}{||overrightarrow{v}|}, \\\\frac{v_{x}}{|overrightarrow{v}|}, \\\\frac{v_{z}}{|overrightarrow{v}|},..\\u0026gt;.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWe kind of go off from the computation rule, and I aplogize for that since I want to fully introduce vector to you (sorry for that I forgot to introduce this at the beginning). So, to save time, I directly write examples of 2D vectors' addition and subtraction (I've explained how they're computed above):\\n\\\\overrightarrow{v_{1}}+\\\\overrightarrow{v_{2}}=\\u0026lt;v_{1,x}+v_{2,x}, v_{1,y}+v_{2,y}\\u0026gt;; \\\\overrightarrow{v_{1}}-\\\\overrightarrow{v_{2}}=\\u0026lt;v_{1,x}-v_{2,x}, v_{1,y}-v_{2,y}\\u0026gt;.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eDon't panick, most of time in physics, we do not need to mathematically calculate it (except sometimes). One property worth knowing is that subtract one vector with another is equivalent as adding this vector with the opposing vector of another. You can prove this on your own, which is very simple.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnother two noteworthy methods of vector addition that help you judge the result is the triangle and parallelogram methods: for triangle method, when you add or subtract one vector with another, you translate the second vector's starting point to be at the end point of the first vector, connecting the line segment from the first vector's starting point to the translated second vector's end point is the resulting new vector; as for parallelogram method, you translate both vectors along another's path, forming a parallelogram, the diagonal vector with starting point at the starting points of the two vectors and end point at the end points of two translated vectors is the resulting new vector.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThen we need to pay special attention to the product rule. It's not the product rule in calculus for derivatives--it's for vectors. There are two kinds of multiplication of vectors, one is dot product, and the other is cross product. Remember that we can write multiplication notation using either cross or dot? It doesn't matter when we're dealing with scalar or normal numbers, yet it will be different due to the introduced multiple variables. Also, I'd like to add on that these two products are more like conventions, especially cross product. You DO NOT to need to understand them (it's indeed highly perceiveable, yet I don't want you to split hairs on this). \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThe result of dot product is a scalar, yet the result of cross product is a new vector; this is the first difference between the two kinds of multiplications.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor v_{1} \\\\cdot v_{2}, the resulting scalar's value is v_{1,x}v_{2,x}+v_{1,y}v_{2,y}. Or, you can write in another way which is more intuitive: /|v_{1}/|/|v_{2}/|/cos(/theta). The notation of \\\"/|/|\\\" means obtaining the magnitude of the the vector, and the angle /theta is the angle between the two vectors (this calculation method is independent of the coordinate system). Therefore, the dot product's geometric meaning is projecting one vector onto another and multiply them. And the reason for that the result of dot product is always a scalar is that it loses the property of a vector when doing the dot product using the first method. For more detailed explanation, go view the mathematics page of dot product and cross product of vectors (I probably will explain with more details, but if not, please forgive me).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor v_{1} \\\\times v_{2}, the process starts with calculating the determinant of this matrix: \\n[\\n\\\\begin{matrix}\\n\\\\widehat{i} \\u0026amp; \\\\widehat{j} \\u0026amp; \\\\widehat{k} \\\\\\nx_{1} \\u0026amp; y_{1} \\u0026amp; z_{1} \\\\\\nx_{2} \\u0026amp; y_{2} \\u0026amp; z_{2}\\n\\\\end{matrix}\\n].\\nSo, the result is v_{3}=(y_{1}z_{2}-z_{1}y_{2})\\\\widehat{i}-(x_{1}z_{2}-z_{1}x_{2})\\\\widehat{j}+(x_{1}y_{2}-x_{2}y_{1})\\\\widehat{k}. This is a new vector that is both perpendicualr to the two previous vectors (so it's perpendicular to the plane that the two vectors are in). And you may notice that for {j}, the beginning notation is negative sign instead of positive sign, and this is because for every even row, the component begins with positive sign. For further information of why this works, go view the mathematics page of dot product and cross product. Meanwhile, since the result of cross product is also a vector, we can obtain its magnitude (which is the usual computation in physics) by the formula: /|v_{1}/|/|v_{2}/|/sin(/theta) where angle /theta is the angle between the two previous vectors. This can be interpreted as calculating the area of the parellelogram enclosed by the two previous vectors (indeed if you use one vector dot the cross product of the other two, the result is a volum enclosed by these three vectors).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSo, the dot product is usually applied in calculating work done or intergrating the electric field over the Gaussian surface; the cross product occurs when you're calculating the angular momentum and the Lorentz force. Both are necessary and basic skills that you need to learn about for further studying physics. As I've said, treat them as conventions by now, and you'll gradually pick up the deeper meaning of these two computations.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFinally, I'd like to clarify or emphasize the key takeaway of this page: the computation including only scalars only has a new scalar as the result; the computation including only vectors can result in either scalar or vector; the computation including vectors and scalars can only result in vector (when you multiply a scalar with a vector, you're changing its magnitude). Yet you can also obtain a scalar of volume doing the scalar triple product: a /cdot (b /times c). That's all. Take your time. For any observed problem of this site, please leave your comment with constructive suggestions, thanks.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Kinematics\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/kinematics/\"}");
